  "body": "## Change and mathematical scope\n\nThis takes Nick Petalas's higher-moment handoff from PR #2181. For the fixed QQ16 `F0/B_1_2` erasure and observation contract, **every read-only rational-linear record of at most fifteen scalars per regular-cover owner fails** to restore the original selected inputs, so at least **sixteen** are necessary.\n\n- **Fourth moment:** for `T = (B0*B0 + B1*B1 + B2*B2)/48` on the actual 96 ordinary ports and owner routes, `tr(T^4) = (1823/36)|G|`, which extends the known moments `(24, 28, 109/3)|G|`.\n- **Rank bound:** the polynomial `p(t) = 1 \u2212 (1 \u2212 81t/40 + 9t\u00b2/10)\u00b2` satisfies `p(0) = 0` and `p(t) \u2264 1`, so `rank([B0;B1;B2]) \u2265 tr p(T) = (117/5)|G|`. Through PR #2153's transfer (`dim J(ker B1) = rank \u2212 8|G|`), the selected A/T defect is at least `\u2308(77/5)|G|\u2309`.\n- **Consequence:** a rank-d record leaves at least `max(0, \u230877|G|/5\u2309 \u2212 d)` dimensions invisible; at fifteen per owner the residual is at least `\u23082|G|/5\u2309 > 0`. This strengthens the necessary width from fourteen (PR #2181) to sixteen. On this erased family, sixteen coordinates, the two records `(B0 X_U, B2 X_U)`, recover the defect, as PR #2153 noted; the broader recovery of this account's PR #2174 keeps its recorded-cut premise.\n- **Status:** a scoped proof with exact finite certificates, author-checked by two different calculations. Information dimension, not paid recursive rank; no supplier or \u03ba change.\n\n**Remaining obligations.** No independent human review or formal-kernel proof; the two calculations share the credited source binder. The inherited erased-family tail and native interpretation are not rerun. Final-only access to the B1 cut, native reads and retention, dirty restoration, hosting, tape, precision, a compact actual-cover null vector and a full supplier replay remain open. Changed first pairs or trajectories, nonlinear encoders and uncounted payload storage are outside the theorem.\n\n## Task and overlap\n\n- **Task ID / scope key:** `joint-query-controller` (`22e535909f1d243e7553`), refined to `QQ16-fixed-F0-B12/three-query-fourth-moment/quartic-transcript-capacity`. Chooser nonce `65b001ccb2c98c18607f8043d2993ce6`, track recursive-interfaces, priority revision 4; the card was the chooser's second suggestion, taken through #2181's released handoff (passing over `centre-path-sharing`, whose nearest handoff this account's PR #2048 covers, and `connector-alphabet-change`, whose nearby cases are covered). No reroll.\n- **Base and exact heads:** base `0fdd7d82de03e43c9e5a1000dfbcd380d1b687d2`, rechecked unchanged at 22:54 UTC; contribution `968ccd289de1130c437cb4936608c4ee419a27de`. PR #2181 `e18ca76987f5945c6a4b5b6bebbe73cdab3c4561`, PR #2104 `4bdc574da394a39f955e8cf4637075649c43a632`, PR #2153 `5646398dcdc01769df83b939c6b3eb5ecb4d5ebd`; PR #1969 is consumed from main with pins in `SOURCES.json`. No pending checkout, network access or third-party module is needed.\n- **Scope status:** changed, from the card's constructed observation to a necessary information bound for a changed record contract; the first pair, ordinary tail and observation contract are fixed.\n- **Nearest work:** PR #2104 (two moments), PR #2153 (the selected-defect transfer), PR #2181 (three moments, excluding thirteen) and this account's PR #2174 (recovery at a recorded cut, a different positive theorem).\n- **Claim and final check:** the claim is in [discussion #2074](https://github.com/CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds/discussions/2074#discussioncomment-18935517). A final overlap check of open PRs and Discussions (complete pagination), plus a live check of newer PRs, found no competing work.\n\n## Validation\n\n```sh\npython3 -I -B scripts/research_package.py --package research/qq16-fourth-moment-capacity\npython3 -I -O -B research/qq16-fourth-moment-capacity/verify.py\npython3 -I -O -B research/qq16-fourth-moment-capacity/oracle.py\n```\n\nLinux x86_64, CPython 3.12.3, standard library. The registered replay (primary certificate, separate block-Gram oracle, semantic and artifact controls, normal and optimized) was run again on the final package bytes before submission, under an 8 GiB memory cap: it passed in about 18 seconds and left the worktree unchanged.\n\n```text\nPASS_QQ16_FOURTH_MOMENT_CAPACITY moment4=1823/36 rank>=117|G|/5 defect>=77|G|/5 width>=16 no_new_kappa\nPASS_QQ16_BLOCK_GRAM_ORACLE moments=24,28,109/3,1823/36 blocks=9\nPASS_REGISTERED_RESEARCH_PACKAGE research/qq16-fourth-moment-capacity outcome=completed\n```\n\n- The fourth moment comes from an exhaustive census of 55,296 ordered route products and, separately, from a row-matrix block-Gram contraction; they agree. `RESULT.json` holds the census, six full-matrix digests, all stage-word counts and the rational coefficients.\n- Eight test groups run fifteen deliberate corruptions in normal and optimized modes; a read-only relocated export passes.\n- I rechecked the polynomial step by hand: with the four moments, `tr p(T) = (81/20)\u00b724 \u2212 (6561/1600 + 9/5)\u00b728 + (729/200)\u00b7(109/3) \u2212 (81/100)\u00b7(1823/36) = 117/5` per `|G|`.\n\n## Credit\n\n- Nick Petalas (@petalas, PR #2181): the third moment and the handoff. Ross Richardson (@RossRichardson, PR #2104): the moment method. Ali Romman (@aliromman, PR #1969): the maps, routes and witness. Cinna Davis and Adam Gall: the adjacent observation results. This account's PR #2153: the selected-defect transfer.\n- Rohan Arun and the retained geometry and route lineage.\n\nNew work is Apache-2.0; retained notices are unchanged.\n\n**AI assistance:** submitted by @commdata2338 with substantial AI assistance. OpenAI Codex (GPT-6 Astra) did the derivation, the implementation, the checking and the writing; it was packaged and submitted with Claude Code. No independent human review is claimed.\n\n## Open next questions\n\nSee the package README and `TASK.json`. The smallest useful follow-ups: decide whether an actual controller's final records recover the B1-cut values (the premise this lower bound and PR #2174's recovery share), and price the sixteen-coordinate record natively. A sharper bound would need the full observation operator, not only this erased family.\n\n## Workflow feedback (optional)\n\n- **What helped:** the short current-status summaries on the board made it easy to find the precise next question and to tell the completed transcript and recovery results apart.\n- **Friction:** the first chooser output was hidden by tool-output truncation and recovered from the session log; no reroll.\n- **Suggestion:** save the chooser's stdout to a local file before displaying a short summary of the nonce, track and suggestions.\n\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01X86sCSXdoDd61SDFfC1PKG\n",
